A||||10||Seanh||What is involved with a 3.4 swap?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-31-2000||09:45 PM||||I was looking around some junkyards today for Autodimming rear view mirror I want, and I can across a 3.4DOHC engine with less then 60K on it. I was wanting to do an engine swap when I rebuild the car, but I have been debating which one. Now that I have found a motor I need to know how much more is involved. I know you use from the heads up from the 2.8 engine. What else has to be done to the car to make this work? What kind of money will I be looking at without the price of the engine included? It is a long block by the way. Is the DOHC 3.4 the best engine to use for this? Or should I look around to find another type? I am going for alot of torque, as I dont race all that much, but I do like beating folks off the lights. And lastly, how much time am I looking at for this swap? Average machanical skills and acess to this forum? Thanks <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||10-31-2000||10:32 PM||||<B>Seanh</B>, the 3.4 DOHC is from a front wheel drive Pontiac. The engine you need is a 3.4 from a '93 - '95 Camero or Firebird - these are the rear wheel drive versions. With the RWD version it is necessary to move the starter to the other side of the engine and then basically everything from the heads up will bolt on from a standard 2.8 V-6 fiero engine. Basically put, the 3.4 RWD version is a fairly simple conversion, the 3.4 FWD version is much more complicated.<P>------------------<BR>                <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                            <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group<BR><A HREF="http://www.http://www.fieroshop.net/" TARGET=_blank>www.http://www.fieroshop.net/</A> - Fiero Shop - Richmond, CA - 1-510-23-FIERO<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 10-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||10-31-2000||10:52 PM||||Oh okay. I knew it was a front wheel drive, but just didnt know how hard it would be to do. I will probally be looking for another engine then. Anyone know of a good place to get them? I am willing to pay shipping if I cant find locally. Also, my car is an automatic tranny, what will I need to do this if anything. Anyone know of a good website about the 3.4 conversion? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||11-01-2000||12:31 AM||||call us at the Fiero Shop (1-510-233-4376) we can explain the steps you need to take in order to install a 3.4............<P>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                           <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group<BR><A HREF="http://www.http://www.fieroshop.net/" TARGET=_blank>www.http://www.fieroshop.net/</A> - Fiero Shop - Richmond, CA - 1-510-23-FIERO||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||11-01-2000||12:53 AM||||O.K. Once and for all, this is what one needs to do for the 3.4 swap. Info. below was taken from the king himself...Ed Parks!<P>A Camaro 3.4 in a Fiero ? <P>We had a drill guide plate manufactured that allows drilling and tapping starter mounting holes on an assembled 3.4 longblock. After that it is simple to redress<BR>it with all the Fiero V6 parts so that it looks EXACTLY like the original engine. The 3.4's have a plug where the distributor installs (even TDC, but head is in the<BR>way, tried that to see if we could run TDC with Fiero computer). Just remove plug, install distributor, and you'll find the distributor hold down bolt hole is even<BR>threaded! There are a few easy bracket modifications to do or that we furnish. The 3.4 needed is used in 93-95 Camaro's and Firebird's. It is also available new<BR>from GM Performance as a replacement for S-10 and Blazer. Larger flow rate injectors have to be used and don't come with the "crate" engine. Would have to be<BR>purchased separately. They will come with engines pulled from wrecked cars.. Must use the 3.4 injectors, or 305 chevy (both will "clip" to the Fiero fuel rail). We<BR>also use an adjustable fuel regulator to be on the safe side. Have found different engines (depending on cam and exhaust headers) like different pressures. There<BR>are a few other minor mods as well as the major one of changing starter sides. We are charging $450 to modify the block. If you are auto, part of the 3.4 needs to be<BR>cutaway to clear the trans. Not necessary for std trans. The bolt hole that takes the bolt from the pass side, lowest bellhousing one toward the trunk, is threaded in<BR>the 3.4 and needs to be drilled smooth. The oil pressure sensor tube needs an adapter, 3.4 blocks are minus one of the bosses that the engine shock mounts to. That<BR>boss is also where the a/c adjusting bracket bolts. Cure is: shock mount bracket with bolt welded from the back to become a stud. 88's will then need 85-87 a/c<BR>bracket. A modified 85-87 front engine mount bracket must be used on the 88 engine cradle if you use the 3.4 oil pan. 87-88 timing covers will work with 3.4 pan,<BR>otherwise use cover and pan from 85-86 2.8. 3.4 dampner needs to aligned with the 2.8, and new timing mark cut. Lower bellhousing bolt hole (trans side) is<BR>threaded on 3.4. Drill out threads for bolt coming from opposite direction to transaxle. Reducer bushing necessary for oil sensor tube. Clip wires from 3.4 cam and<BR>crank sensor and leave installed, is easiest way to plug these holes. (crate engines come with plugs) Remove 3.4 oil cooler, and install 2.8 filter adapter. 3.4's need<BR>neutrally balanced flexplate/flywheel which 88 2.8's already have. A 4-5 lb heavier flywheel available for 3.4TDC from Chev for about 1/3 the cost of 88 Fiero.<BR>(They are identical except for weight). An 87-88 Fiero timing chain cover will work with the 3.4 oil pan. You would need to use both your 85 or 86 pan and cover,<BR>as a set. It also requires a Fiero water pump instead of the 3.4's. Most folks want a new water pump installed, plugs, wires, belts, maybe a clutch at this time. Since<BR>the cost of transferring all the old parts is included, there is no further labor charge if they are new parts, just the cost of any new parts you might want/need. Can't<BR>give an exact price, since it would depend on you, and the purchase cost of the 3.4 ||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||11-01-2000||01:24 AM||||Sean,<P>There are several 3.4's here in Denver for sale.  You may want to take a cruise down here.<P>If you plan on rebuilding it you can get a FWD 3.4 (from a newer Grand Am) and build it with the flat top pistons and iron heads.  The starter is on the correct side, so that takes any drilling out of the equation.  If you don't want that hassle then a 3.4 Camaro/Firebird motor is in order.<P>The DOHC 3.4 has no provisions for lifters (no lifter galley), so it is no good for a swap (as far as looking like a Fiero motor). <P>However, you can install the entire DOHC motor in your Fiero (many have already done this).  It is a major swap.....not as easy as the "regular" 3.4 swap.<P>At any rate.....LMK if you want me to locate one here for you......they run about $750 complete.    ||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||11-01-2000||03:19 AM||||yep, everything they have told you here is accurate. the only other thing we do here at the Fiero Shop is, if it is a manual tranny, we use our kevlar clutch and an aluminum flywheel. A much better set-up than the stock version. Also, the 3.4 RWD version appears the same as a 2.8 making it much easier to smog here in CA -- but, you get the added horsepower and torque. Ask Ed about our kevlar clutch and aluminum flywheel!<P>the cost to do the swap is right on the money too - our cost is the same as Ed's -- dollar for performance I think you will find the 3.4 from a '93-'95 Firebird or Camaro is your best bet. We are also doing 2.5 to 3.4 conversions but they are a wee bit more complicated.<P>------------------<BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/tgowens_sig.gif">                           <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A> - Blue '87 GT - Gold '87 GT<BR><A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> - Classic Fiero Owners Group<BR><A HREF="http://www.http://www.fieroshop.net/" TARGET=_blank>www.http://www.fieroshop.net/</A> - Fiero Shop - Richmond, CA - 1-510-23-FIERO||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||11-01-2000||05:23 PM||||Okay, I think I pretty much understand it all now. If I could possibly avoid it, I would like not to rebuild the engine. I will probally end up getting one out of Denver. I will have a pain in a** getting it back here though. I have a truck, 85 Ford Bronco with 266K on it and it burns more oil then gas. Not to mention getting 6-7 MPG, and a trailer to haul it in, but, I kinda hate to take my chances with the truck. I am thinking everyday that it will probally throw a rod. If I possibly can, I would like to make it down there before x-mas to check out motors. I am going to start on the car the first week of Jan and would like to have the motor ready, with the exception of the heads up, to go in the Fiero. Could someone point me in the direction of Ed Parks site, I have seen it, but didnt bookmark it. Hate to have to spend $150.00 on a drill quide though. I will go check motors here locally first and then on to Denver. Anyone have any idea on what it would cost to have an engine shipped here? Thanks <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/singnit3_1.gif">   <BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
